Write a long double 0.0 constant without the host's bytes.

tcc emits a long double constant by copying the host long double, which
only works when the host type is at least as wide as the target's. On an
aarch64 macOS build host long double is 64-bit, so building libtcc1 for
any target with a wider one died with "can't cross compile long double
constants". The only such constant in libtcc1.c and lib-arm64.c is the
implicit 0.0L of comparisons, and 0.0L is all-zero bytes everywhere.
Write LDOUBLE_SIZE zero bytes for it; any other constant still errors.

--- a/tccgen.c
+++ b/tccgen.c
@@ -7776,8 +7776,8 @@
                     memcpy(ptr, &vtop->c.ld, LDOUBLE_SIZE);
                 else if (sizeof(double) == LDOUBLE_SIZE)
                     *(double*)ptr = (double)vtop->c.ld;
-                else if (0 == memcmp(ptr, &vtop->c.ld, LDOUBLE_SIZE))
-                    ; /* nothing to do for 0.0 */
+                else if (vtop->c.ld == 0.0)
+                    memset(ptr, 0, LDOUBLE_SIZE); /* 0.0L is all-zero on every target; host long double may be narrower */
 #ifndef TCC_CROSS_TEST
                 else
                     tcc_error("can't cross compile long double constants");
